CAT = "ukcareSafeguard"; SEG = "SAF"
# Standard 10 Adult safeguarding, Standard 11 Safeguarding children
Q = [
("Which law sets out the duties of councils in England to safeguard adults at risk?",
 "The Care Act 2014", ["The Children Act 1989", "The Mental Health Act 1983", "The Human Rights Act 1998"],
 "The Care Act 2014 puts adult safeguarding on a legal footing, including the council's duty to make enquiries, known as section 42 enquiries."),
("Under the Care Act 2014, which of these is part of the definition of an adult at risk?",
 "They have care and support needs", ["They live alone and are over 75", "They have no family nearby", "They receive a state pension"],
 "Safeguarding duties apply to an adult who has needs for care and support, is experiencing or at risk of abuse or neglect, and as a result of those needs cannot protect themselves."),
("Which of these is one of the six safeguarding principles in the Care Act statutory guidance?",
 "Empowerment", ["Punishment", "Secrecy", "Cost-efficiency"],
 "The six principles are empowerment, prevention, proportionality, protection, partnership and accountability."),
("What does \"Making Safeguarding Personal\" mean?",
 "Safeguarding led by the person", ["Each worker keeps their own safeguarding file", "Families make all safeguarding decisions", "Safeguarding is handled only by police"],
 "Making Safeguarding Personal means involving the person, asking what outcomes they want and supporting them to make choices about their own safety."),
("A resident's savings are being spent by a relative without her agreement. What type of abuse could this be?",
 "Financial abuse", ["Organisational abuse", "Discriminatory abuse", "Self-neglect"],
 "Financial or material abuse includes theft, fraud, pressure over wills or money, and misuse of property or benefits."),
("Which of these is a possible sign of physical abuse?",
 "Unexplained bruises", ["Asking for a later bedtime", "Choosing to eat alone", "Wanting a new hairstyle"],
 "Unexplained bruises, burns or injuries, or injuries in different stages of healing, may be signs of physical abuse and must be reported."),
("A service keeps strict routines that suit staff, such as everyone in bed by 8 pm. What type of abuse could this be?",
 "Organisational abuse", ["Financial abuse", "Discriminatory abuse", "Psychological abuse"],
 "Organisational or institutional abuse is poor care or rigid routines across a service that put staff convenience before people's needs and choices."),
("Which of these is an example of neglect?",
 "Withholding prescribed medicines", ["Offering a choice of meals", "Supporting a hospital visit", "Respecting a refusal of care"],
 "Neglect and acts of omission include ignoring medical or physical care needs, withholding medicines, food or heating."),
("What is self-neglect?",
 "Not caring for one's own health", ["Refusing to see one visitor", "Choosing a different diet", "Spending savings on hobbies"],
 "Self-neglect covers a wide range of behaviour where a person does not care for their own hygiene, health or surroundings, including hoarding."),
("Which of these is a type of abuse listed in the Care Act statutory guidance?",
 "Modern slavery", ["Poor spelling", "Late payment", "Untidy bedrooms"],
 "The guidance lists physical, domestic, sexual, psychological, financial or material, modern slavery, discriminatory, organisational, neglect and acts of omission, and self-neglect."),
("A person tells you a staff member has been hurting them. What should you do?",
 "Listen, reassure and report it", ["Promise to keep it a secret", "Ask the staff member about it", "Wait until you have proof"],
 "Listen calmly, take it seriously, do not promise secrecy, do not investigate yourself, and report straight away following your safeguarding policy."),
("When a person discloses abuse, what kind of questions should you avoid?",
 "Leading questions", ["Open questions", "Gentle questions", "Clarifying questions"],
 "Leading questions can suggest answers and affect any later investigation. Let the person speak, and record what they say in their own words."),
("How should you record a disclosure of abuse?",
 "In their own words, dated", ["In your summary of what happened", "Only in your own diary", "A week later when it is calm"],
 "Record what was said using the person's own words, what you saw, the date and time and who you reported to. Keep to facts, not opinions."),
("What should you do if you report a safeguarding concern and nothing seems to be done?",
 "Escalate it to a higher level", ["Drop it as you have done your part", "Post about it online", "Tell other residents"],
 "If a concern is not acted on, escalate it to a senior manager, the local authority safeguarding team or CQC. Whistleblowing law protects workers who raise concerns in the right way."),
("Which law protects workers who report wrongdoing at work in the public interest?",
 "The Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998", ["The Data Protection Act 2018", "The Care Standards Act 2000", "The Health and Social Care Act 2008"],
 "The Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998 protects workers who make a qualifying disclosure, often called whistleblowing, from unfair treatment."),
("Which is an example of a restrictive practice?",
 "Locking a door to keep someone in", ["Offering a choice of drinks", "Helping someone get to a club", "Knocking before entering a room"],
 "Restrictive practices limit a person's movement or freedom. They must be lawful, proportionate, the least restrictive option and recorded."),
("A person is being pressured online by someone asking for money. What risk is this?",
 "Online financial abuse", ["Organisational abuse", "A data protection breach", "A health and safety hazard"],
 "Technology brings risks such as scams, online grooming and exploitation. The 2025 standards expanded coverage of technology-related risks."),
("Who should you first report a safeguarding concern to at work?",
 "Your manager or safeguarding lead", ["The person's next-door neighbour", "A reporter at the local paper", "Another resident you trust"],
 "Follow your safeguarding policy, which normally means reporting to your manager or the named safeguarding lead. If they are involved, go to the local authority or CQC."),
("Up to what age is a person classed as a child for safeguarding purposes in England?",
 "Under 18", ["Under 16", "Under 14", "Under 21"],
 "Everyone under 18 is a child for child protection purposes, even if they live independently or are in work."),
("Which statutory guidance sets out how agencies in England work together to protect children?",
 "Working Together to Safeguard Children", ["Making Safeguarding Personal", "The Care and Support Statutory Guidance", "Keeping Children Safe at Home"],
 "Working Together to Safeguard Children is the statutory guidance on inter-agency working to safeguard and promote the welfare of children."),
("You visit an adult at home and see that a child there seems frightened and has burns. What should you do?",
 "Report it straight away", ["Leave it, you only support adults", "Ask the child to show you", "Wait for the next visit"],
 "Everyone who works with adults or children has a responsibility to safeguard children. Report concerns about a child promptly using agreed procedures."),
("Which of these is a type of child abuse?",
 "Emotional abuse", ["Strict bedtimes", "Healthy meals", "Homework rules"],
 "The main categories are physical, emotional and sexual abuse and neglect. Child exploitation and online abuse also fall within them."),
("Which of these is a sign that a child may be neglected?",
 "Often hungry and poorly dressed", ["Has a few untidy toys at home", "Sometimes sulks after school", "Prefers to play outside"],
 "Signs of neglect include persistent hunger, poor hygiene, clothes unsuited to the weather, missed health appointments and being left alone."),
("If you think a child is in immediate danger, what should you do?",
 "Call 999", ["Email your manager", "Write it in a diary", "Wait until the next day"],
 "If a child or adult is in immediate danger, call the police on 999. Then follow your organisation's procedures."),
("What is the safest attitude to take about who might abuse someone?",
 "Anyone could be an abuser", ["Only strangers abuse people", "Staff never abuse people", "Family members never abuse"],
 "Abuse can be carried out by anyone, including family, friends, staff, volunteers and other people using services. Keep an open mind."),
("What should you do if someone you suspect of abuse asks you what was reported?",
 "Say nothing, tell your manager", ["Show them a copy of the report", "Warn them to stop what they do", "Tell them who reported it"],
 "Telling the alleged abuser could put the person at further risk or affect an investigation. Pass the approach on to your manager."),
("Which of these is psychological abuse?",
 "Threats and humiliation", ["A fall from a chair", "A meal missed in error", "A change in medicine dose"],
 "Psychological abuse includes threats, humiliation, controlling behaviour, intimidation, isolation and verbal abuse."),
("Which type of abuse involves treating someone badly because of their race or disability?",
 "Discriminatory abuse", ["Organisational abuse", "Self-neglect", "Psychological abuse"],
 "Discriminatory abuse includes harassment, slurs or unequal treatment because of a protected characteristic such as race, disability, age or sexuality."),
]
